A||||10||gomobile||Replacing old DeckLid lift with new KIT ||||6||
Z||000000||gomobile||09-19-2001||05:38 PM||||Has anyone replaced the old trunk LIFTS and replaced them with something else? I need to replace mine (GT model) but want to know if there is anything else besides the one's offer by Rodney Dickman. ||||reg||6
Z||000001||CC Rider||09-19-2001||05:45 PM||||I did mine out of the junkyard off an old Pinto. You just have to find the brackets that work and keep to a support that is around 17" in length or once you get your bracket installed it will be hard to compress to install.||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||09-19-2001||08:22 PM||||Get rodney's, there dosen't need to be anything else  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
Z||000003||qwikgta||09-19-2001||08:48 PM||||Not sure what you mean by lifts.  I did up a homemade decklid strut and I did it for under $3.00.  All you need is a 84 Buick Regal front hood strut (mine was from a junkyard, they gave it to me).  Attach the narrow end of the strut to the decklid.  Use the driverside bolt closest to you as you look at it from the rear.  Then attach the wide end to the unused bracket that is hidden by the thick heat pad on the driverside strut area ((see pics)).  You are done.  If you want to remove the stock lid tensioner then this will still hold your hood up.  If this is confusing email me and I will explain more.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/strut_closeup.jpg">  <BR>After I removed the stock bolt 4-5 times I stripped the threads, this is what I did to fix it.  This is not common that I know of.<P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/strutshot_1.jpg">  <BR>Only shot I have showing the mounting spot on the lower bolt.  If you look on your car you should find a unused bracket in this area.<P>Rob<P><p>[This message has been edited by qwikgta (edited 09-19-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||gomobile||09-20-2001||11:25 AM||||OOPS I meant struts and yes that sounds right if I don't have to spend the 100.00 (rodney dickman price) then why not. I will visit the local salvage place (nicely put) and see what I can walk with.  ||||reg||6
Z||000005||Philphine||09-20-2001||05:33 PM||||mine is similar to qwikgta's except i made a small bracket up around the strut/hood vent area. the next time i'll go with his spot since it's already there.<P>------------------<BR>Phil T.<P>start where you are,<BR>use what you have,<BR>do what you can.||||reg||1
Z||000006||GodSend||09-20-2001||07:21 PM||||Saw someone at the GTA show who used the button release type bar from the hood and put it in the back, exact same spot where it was suggested to put the strut.  The nice thing about this way is that you never really have to worry about the bar losing its holding power  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FieroJoe||09-20-2001||10:31 PM||||Neat idea, except that that bracket is used for the cruise control wiring harness for cars so equipped.  It's just one of those snap type fasteners that keeps the loom neat and tidy, Im sure if you have cruise and put the strut in it might be fine.  Hmm, I may try this.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Voytek||09-21-2001||02:27 PM||||I've used one off a Skylark (I believe), mid-80's and the other day I picked one up for a friend from and 80's Caddy (two short ones on either side).  The Skylark is just right and the Caddy was about 1 inch shorter (i.e. also just fine).  Each one cost $6 CDN (about $4 US).  <P>I have an '87 GT and this is how I attached it:<BR>The Skylark strut came with a bolt that had a flat portion (no thread) where it fits into the hole/hook on the strut.  This end (the thick/cylinder end) I attached to the bracket that holds the rear vent grille on the left.  All you need to do there is pull one of the 2 bolts from the brackett and replace it with this special bolt.  I added a couple of washers so the strut is aligned perfect with the trunk lid.<P>On the lid side, I removed on of the bolts and bought a longer one with a nut.  I put the bolt through the hole/hook on the other end of the strut, threaded the nut onto the bolt WITHOUT tightening it (to allow for strut movement when you open/close the lid) and threaded the bolt into the trunk lid, tightening it with the nut.  One or two washers were also needed for proper alignment.<P>I hope this helps.  Oh, the total costs, with a new bolt, were $6.40 CDN.  Works awesome.<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/voyteks_sig2.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000009||SLYGUY||09-21-2001||11:45 PM||||I will do that too Voytek. <P>My decklid will not stay up either, and neither one of my "repair" manuals talks about resetting the tension to fix this.<BR>Sounds like a good fix to me.<P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000010||FLASHY||09-22-2001||01:45 PM||||Here's what Voytek was talking about. I just did the same thing yesterday. It works great!<P><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/trunkshock2.jpg"> <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/flash_sig.gif"> <P>"I know you believe you understand what you think I wrote, but I'm not sure you realize that what you read is not what I really meant"||||reg||1
